course: technology
lesson: 14
title: Electricity at home: switches, plugs, fuses and alarms
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Electricity reaches your home through a cable, passes the meter that counts it, and goes to the fuse box, which sends it along hidden wires to every switch and socket.
explain: A switch is just a gate in a wire: flick it one way and the path is joined so electricity flows; flick it back and the path is broken.
explain: Mains electricity is strong enough to kill, so homes are full of guards: shutters inside sockets, fuses that melt, and switches in the fuse box that cut the power in a blink.
explain: Think of the evening at home: the lights come on, the Wi-Fi blinks, the smoke alarm sits quietly on the ceiling waiting, and all of it hangs on those hidden wires.
explain: The rules are short: hands dry, nothing into sockets, pull the plug not the cable, and anything broken or scorched is for an adult or an electrician.
fact: A light switch joins or breaks the wire to the light; in UK bathrooms lights often use a pull cord so that wet hands do not touch a switch.
fact: An LED bulb makes light from tiny crystals, with little heat, so it uses much less power and lasts years longer than an old glowing-wire bulb.
fact: A UK plug has three pins; the longer top one is the earth pin, which opens little shutters in the socket, and a UK plug has a fuse inside.
fact: A socket is the end of wires from the fuse box, and its shutters cover the holes until a plug's earth pin opens them.
fact: A fuse is a thin wire that melts if too much current (flow of electricity) flows, breaking the circuit (the loop of wire the electricity runs round) before the wires overheat; the number on it tells how many amps it allows.
fact: The fuse box, or consumer unit, has circuit breakers that switch off a circuit when too much current flows, and an RCD that switches off in about four hundredths of a second if electricity leaks, for example through a person.
fact: Only adults reset a trip, and if it keeps tripping, an electrician checks it.
fact: The electricity meter counts how much the home uses in kilowatt-hours, and a smart meter sends the reading automatically; a kettle uses more power than a whole room of LED lights.
fact: A smoke alarm senses smoke and beeps very loudly; a short chirp every so often means its battery is low, and it should be tested each month with its button; never take its battery out, but tell an adult to change it.
fact: A carbon monoxide alarm senses carbon monoxide, a poison gas with no colour, smell or taste that can come from badly burning boilers, fires or cookers.
fact: If the smoke alarm sounds for a real fire: get out, stay out, and call the fire brigade; if the carbon monoxide alarm beeps, get out and tell an adult to call for help.
fact: A Wi-Fi router is a small radio station that talks to your devices by radio and passes data along a cable to the internet; thick walls, mirrors and fish tanks can weaken the signal.
fact: A doorbell camera sends video over Wi-Fi to a phone when someone presses the button or moves nearby, but children still let an adult answer the door to strangers.
fact: Never touch switches with wet hands, never put anything into a socket, and hold the plug, not the cable, to pull it out.
exercise: What does a light switch do? | Joins or breaks the wire so electricity can flow or not | Stores electricity for later | Makes electricity from nothing | Cools the wire down
exercise: Why do UK bathrooms often have a pull cord for the light? | So wet hands don't touch a switch | Because switches rust quickly in all the steam | Because cords are cheaper than proper switches | So small children can't reach up and play with it
exercise: Why is an LED bulb nearly cool when an old bulb gets hot? | It turns more of the electricity into light, not heat | It has a tiny fan hidden inside that blows the heat away | It is made of ice-cold glass | It only works for a few minutes
exercise: Why is the top pin on a UK plug longer? | It is the earth pin and opens the socket's shutters | It carries twice as much electricity as the other two pins | It is just there to hold the plug | It stops the plug falling out upside down
exercise: How many pins does a UK plug have? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 1
exercise: Why do sockets have little shutters inside? | To cover the holes until a plug's earth pin opens them | To keep the electricity warm | To stop the dust getting dirty | To make a clicking sound
exercise: What happens to a fuse when too much current flows? | Its thin wire melts and breaks the circuit | It makes more electricity | It turns the lights brighter | It sends a text message to the electrician to come
exercise: Why is it good that a fuse breaks? | It stops the wires overheating and starting a fire | It saves the plug from wearing out too quickly over the years | It makes the machine work faster | It lets you know the bill is due
exercise: What does an RCD in the fuse box do? | Switches off in a split second if electricity leaks, like through a person | Counts how much electricity you use | Keeps the Wi-Fi running in a power cut | Turns the heating on at night
exercise: The electricity trips and goes off in half the house. Who resets it? | An adult; and if it keeps tripping, an electrician | Any child who can reach | Nobody, it fixes itself next week once the wires have cooled | The neighbour next door
exercise: What does the electricity meter do? | Counts how much electricity the home uses | Makes electricity for the home | Stops the electricity coming in during a storm | Switches off the lights at night
exercise: What does a smoke alarm's short chirp every so often mean? | Its battery is low | There is a small fire | It has smelled toast | It is working perfectly
exercise: How often should a smoke alarm be tested with its button? | Every month | Once in ten years | Only when there is a fire | Never, it tests itself
exercise: Why do we need an alarm for carbon monoxide? | It has no colour, smell or taste | It smells too bad for anyone to stay near | It only ever comes out of boilers at night | It makes a loud hissing noise you can't miss
exercise: What is a Wi-Fi router? | A small radio station for your devices and the internet | A box that makes electricity | A camera that watches the house | A speaker for music
exercise: Why can Wi-Fi be bad in some rooms? | Thick walls, mirrors and fish tanks weaken the signal | The Wi-Fi gets tired upstairs | Wi-Fi only travels in straight lines going downwards from the box | Rooms with carpets block all signals
exercise: The doorbell camera shows a stranger. What do you do? | Let an adult answer the door | Open it and ask who they are | Open it a little to look | Shout your name through the door
exercise: Our kitchen has 4 double sockets. How many holes for plugs is that? | 8 | 4 | 6 | 12
test: smoke alarm keeps chirping and it's driving me mad, just yank the battery out? | No, never take it out; tell an adult to change it | Yes, it only really needs a battery at night when you sleep | Yes, it's not important | No, smash it instead
test: In a film a boy pokes a fork into a socket and his hair stands up, and he is fine. What's true? | Never put anything into a socket: mains electricity can kill | It is safe with a plastic fork | It is safe if the light is off | Only metal forks are a problem
test: why does the alarm go off when toast burns lol it's not a fire? | It senses smoke, and burnt toast makes smoke | It hears the toaster pop | It feels the kitchen getting warm and thinks it is a fire | It smells the butter
test: My cousin says the fuse in a plug is there to make the plug heavier. Right? | No, it melts to cut the power if too much current flows | Yes, to stop it falling out | Yes, it is a weight | No, it is to make the plug light up
test: Which uses more electricity: a kettle, or all the LED lights in a room? | The kettle | The LED lights | They use exactly the same | Neither uses any
test: What would happen if a frayed wire let electricity leak through someone touching it, in a house with an RCD? | The RCD would switch the power off in a split second | The RCD would make the current stronger to push it through | Nothing at all would change | The meter would stop counting
test: The carbon monoxide alarm is beeping. What do you do? | Get out and tell an adult to call for help | Open the fridge to cool the gas | Go back to sleep, it's most likely a false alarm | Sniff around for the smell
test: ugh my hands are wet, can I just flick the light on? | No, dry your hands first | Yes, light switches are waterproof | Yes, if you use one finger | No, but you can use your elbow
test: Leo yanks the plug out by its cable. What is the rule? | Hold the plug, not the cable | It's fine if you pull fast | Pull with two hands on the cable | Wiggle the cable side to side
test: Why does a doorbell camera ring the phone even before the button is pressed? | It sensed someone moving near the door | It guesses when visitors come | It hears footsteps from far away | It is connected to the street lights outside the house
test: Mum says she pays for electricity in kilowatt-hours. What counts them? | The electricity meter | The fuse box | The Wi-Fi router | The smoke alarm
exam: Identify the function of the fuse contained within a United Kingdom mains plug. | It melts so as to interrupt an excessive current | It stores electrical energy for later use | It increases the voltage supplied to the appliance | It records the energy consumed by the appliance
exam: It is given that a residual current device detects a leakage of current through a person. State the response of the device. | It disconnects the supply within a fraction of a second | It increases the current to the circuit | It sounds an alarm but leaves the supply connected | It records the event and takes no further action
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises carbon monoxide? | A poisonous gas without colour, odour or taste | A harmless gas with a strong smell of rotten eggs | A visible grey smoke from burning food | A gas produced only by electric heaters
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the intermittent chirp of a smoke alarm. | It indicates that the battery requires replacement | It indicates that a fire is in progress | It indicates that the alarm has become fully charged | It indicates that the alarm should be removed
